From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] a gap in scheduling cannot be read as a change of the clock

UsageStatsService decides that somebody set the clock by reading the two clocks
one statement after the other and comparing how far they have moved apart:

    final long actualSystemTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
    final long actualRealtime = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
    final long expectedSystemTime = (actualRealtime - mRealTimeSnapshot) + mSystemTimeSnapshot;
    final long diffSystemTime = actualSystemTime - expectedSystemTime;

Nothing holds the two reads together. If the thread is taken off the CPU for D
between them, the wall clock is read at t and the monotonic clock at t+D, so
diffSystemTime comes out at about -D and a gap of more than two seconds reads
as a clock change. The skewed pair is then stored as the new snapshot, which
makes the next undisturbed call come out at about +D, and the skew is carried
into the timestamp of every event recorded until the next correction
(convertToSystemTimeLocked). On a phone this cannot happen: no thread waits two
seconds between two adjacent statements. On one 1 GHz Cortex-A9 with a
compilation running it can, and what the service does about it -- reload the
whole of the usage statistics and post another check of the idle states -- costs
the core it is already short of.

This device logged exactly that on 2026-10-20 while dex2oat had the core:

    Time changed in UsageStats by 2 seconds
    Time changed in UsageStats by -3 seconds
    Time changed in UsageStats by 4 seconds

and system_server held 85% of the core for over thirty minutes, with the thread
dump (SIGQUIT, /data/anr/trace_NN) in UsageStatsService.onStatsReloaded calling
postOneTimeCheckIdleStates. That the gaps are what started it is the reading
that fits -- the alternating signs are what a skewed snapshot produces, and the
machine was at 100% CPU throughout -- but one log and one thread dump do not
establish it, and a real clock being set is not ruled out. What this patch
fixes is the part that is certain from the source: the comparison cannot tell
the two apart, and acts anyway.

The wall clock is read somewhere between the two readings of the monotonic one,
so the offset between the clocks is known only to within the width of that
pair. Reading the monotonic clock on both sides gives the interval the offset
lies in -- and the snapshot it is compared against was taken the same way, so
it has an interval too and the comparison is between two of them. The snapshot
therefore has to carry its own width; comparing a fresh interval against a
point that pretends to be exact reintroduces the fault one step later, which is
what a first version of this patch did:

    a real jump of 10 s seen through a 6 s wide sample gives [4, 10], correctly
    acted on; the midpoint then stores an offset 3 s short of the truth, and the
    next undisturbed call reads that 3 s as a second clock change.

With both widths carried, the test is whether the whole difference interval is
past the threshold:

  - A gap in scheduling, with no clock change, always leaves zero inside the
    difference, whichever side of either wall-clock read the gap falls on and
    however wide either sample is. It is never acted on.
  - A clock somebody set by more than the threshold plus both widths lies
    outside the interval entirely and is acted on at once. This is why the test
    is an interval rather than a maximum width: a rule that threw wide samples
    away would, on a device whose samples are always wide, never correct a clock
    that really was set.
  - What is left is a change between the threshold and the threshold plus the
    widths, which waits for a reading narrow enough to show it.

So that waiting ends, a call that finds no change and a tighter reading than the
snapshot carries re-anchors the snapshot to it. The snapshot only ever narrows
there, and a wide one left in place would go on hiding changes a narrow one
would show. The point value kept for converting event timestamps
(convertToSystemTimeLocked) is the middle of the interval, which is the estimate
with the smallest worst-case error.

--- a/services/usage/java/com/android/server/usage/UsageStatsService.java
+++ b/services/usage/java/com/android/server/usage/UsageStatsService.java
@@ -151,6 +151,13 @@
     private File mUsageStatsDir;
     long mRealTimeSnapshot;
     long mSystemTimeSnapshot;
+    /**
+     * PW3: how far apart the two readings that took the snapshot above were. The
+     * wall clock was read somewhere inside that span, so the offset the snapshot
+     * stands for is known only to within it, and checkAndGetTimeLocked() has to
+     * carry that forward or it will read its own imprecision as a clock change.
+     */
+    long mRealTimeSnapshotSpread;
 
     boolean mAppIdleEnabled;
     boolean mAppIdleTempParoled;
@@ -223,8 +230,11 @@
             mAppIdleHistory = new AppIdleHistory(SystemClock.elapsedRealtime());
         }
 
-        mRealTimeSnapshot = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
+        final long beforeRealtime = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
         mSystemTimeSnapshot = System.currentTimeMillis();
+        final long afterRealtime = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
+        mRealTimeSnapshot = beforeRealtime + (afterRealtime - beforeRealtime) / 2;
+        mRealTimeSnapshotSpread = afterRealtime - beforeRealtime;
 
         publishLocalService(UsageStatsManagerInternal.class, new LocalService());
         publishBinderService(Context.USAGE_STATS_SERVICE, new BinderService());
@@ -669,12 +679,30 @@
      * This should be the only way to get the time from the system.
      */
     private long checkAndGetTimeLocked() {
+        // PW3: the wall clock is read between these two readings of the monotonic
+        // one, so the offset between the two clocks is known only to within the
+        // width of the pair -- and on this hardware a thread can be off the CPU
+        // for seconds between two statements.
+        final long beforeRealtime = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
         final long actualSystemTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
-        final long actualRealtime = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
-        final long expectedSystemTime = (actualRealtime - mRealTimeSnapshot) + mSystemTimeSnapshot;
-        final long diffSystemTime = actualSystemTime - expectedSystemTime;
-        if (Math.abs(diffSystemTime) > TIME_CHANGE_THRESHOLD_MILLIS
+        final long afterRealtime = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
+        // The snapshot was taken the same way and carries its own width, so what
+        // is compared are two intervals. Their difference always contains zero
+        // when the clock did not change, whatever either width is; a clock that
+        // was set by more than the threshold and both widths falls outside it.
+        final long snapshotHalf = mRealTimeSnapshotSpread / 2;
+        final long diffAtLatest = (actualSystemTime - snapshotHalf)
+                - ((afterRealtime - mRealTimeSnapshot) + mSystemTimeSnapshot);
+        final long diffAtEarliest = (actualSystemTime + snapshotHalf)
+                - ((beforeRealtime - mRealTimeSnapshot) + mSystemTimeSnapshot);
+        if ((diffAtLatest > TIME_CHANGE_THRESHOLD_MILLIS
+                        || diffAtEarliest < -TIME_CHANGE_THRESHOLD_MILLIS)
                 && ENABLE_TIME_CHANGE_CORRECTION) {
+            final long actualRealtime =
+                    beforeRealtime + (afterRealtime - beforeRealtime) / 2;
+            final long expectedSystemTime =
+                    (actualRealtime - mRealTimeSnapshot) + mSystemTimeSnapshot;
+            final long diffSystemTime = actualSystemTime - expectedSystemTime;
             // The time has changed.
             Slog.i(TAG, "Time changed in UsageStats by " + (diffSystemTime / 1000) + " seconds");
             final int userCount = mUserState.size();
@@ -684,6 +712,37 @@
             }
             mRealTimeSnapshot = actualRealtime;
             mSystemTimeSnapshot = actualSystemTime;
+            mRealTimeSnapshotSpread = afterRealtime - beforeRealtime;
+        } else if (afterRealtime - beforeRealtime < mRealTimeSnapshotSpread) {
+            // PW3: nothing changed, and this reading is tighter than the one the
+            // snapshot carries. Take it: the snapshot only ever narrows here, and
+            // a wide one left in place would go on hiding changes that a narrow
+            // one would show.
+            //
+            // Re-measured, and what that costs is worth writing down. The pair is
+            // what convertToSystemTimeLocked() turns a monotonic event time into a
+            // wall time with, and onTimeChanged() -- which is not called on this
+            // path -- is what moves the statistics already stored. So taking the
+            // wall clock just read moves the first without the second, by however
+            // much this reading implies, and nothing afterwards makes it up.
+            //
+            // It is still right, because that difference is the correction. A
+            // reading taken across a wide gap leaves the pair off by up to half
+            // that width; the firing that took it told the statistics an amount
+            // off by the same half-width, so pair and statistics agreed. This
+            // branch takes the error out of the pair, which is what keeps the next
+            // comparison honest, and leaves it in the statistics, bounded by half
+            // the widest reading there has been -- seconds, once.
+            //
+            // Carrying the offset across instead, so that the pair never moves
+            // without a correction, was tried on 2026-10-21 and is worse: the
+            // error stays in the pair, the next narrow reading sees it as a
+            // difference of half the old width, and the detector fires on it. That
+            // is a false detection per narrowing, and a false detection is a flush
+            // of every user's statistics -- the thing this patch exists to stop.
+            mRealTimeSnapshot = beforeRealtime + (afterRealtime - beforeRealtime) / 2;
+            mSystemTimeSnapshot = actualSystemTime;
+            mRealTimeSnapshotSpread = afterRealtime - beforeRealtime;
         }
         return actualSystemTime;
     }
